{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"EXECUTION DECISION (recorded before any code moves) — how the move interacts with J1's unmodifiable oracle.\n\nJ1's characterization file imports THREE symbols from their pre-move paths:\n  line 286: from rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira.probe import classify_probe_response\n  line 324: from rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira.probe import RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES\n  line 333: from rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira.jira_fields import _RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK\nAC1 requires J1's ASSERTIONS pass unmodified, and another AC forbids re-export shims at pre-move paths. Resolved per-symbol rather than with one blanket rule:\n\n1. RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES (line 324) — NO CHANGE NEEDED. Already decided in the remediated plan: the frozenset is Cloud's configured VALUE and stays defined in adapters/jira/probe.py. J1's pin holds verbatim.\n\n2. classify_probe_response (line 286) — Cloud-side BOUND FUNCTION, not a shim. adapters/jira/probe.py keeps a callable classify_probe_response(issue_key, status_code, payload) that delegates to the relocated jira_family classifier, passing Cloud's RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES as the required keyword-only resolved-status set. This is NOT a bare re-export: it BINDS a Cloud-specific configured value, exactly the pattern the plan already sanctions for _sanitize_description/_sanitize_comment (jira_fields.py survives as the Cloud-side contract-construction site). Consequence: J1's import line AND its call AND its assertions are all untouched, and probe.py's own probe() uses the same bound function. The shared CLASSIFIER still has exactly one implementation, in jira_family/.\n\n3. _RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK (line 333) — IMPORT LINE RE-POINTED. This one is pure data with no Cloud-specific binding to construct, and jira_fields.py does not itself consume it (its only consumer is backend.py:275). Leaving a name bound in jira_fields.py purely so an old import resolves WOULD be the re-export shim the AC forbids. So J1's line 333 is re-pointed to adapters/jira_family/ while the assertion body is preserved VERBATIM — permitted because AC1 is scoped to J1's ASSERTIONS, and the plan's standing rule is that every internal importer is re-pointed in this same change (the same treatment just applied to test_config.py's by-path parity test).\n\nNet: exactly one J1 line changes (an import), zero J1 assertions change, and no shim is introduced.\n\nSECOND EXECUTION DECISION — how 'acli*.py change ONLY in import lines' is satisfiable given the move to PUBLIC names. acli.py's __all__ (lines 86-103) lists _sanitize_comment/_sanitize_label/_JIRA_LABEL_MAX_CHARS/_JIRA_SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS/InvalidLabelError, and ~8 call sites use the underscore names. If jira_family exports public names, a naive edit would have to rewrite __all__ and every call site, breaking that AC. Resolution: the ACLI modules use ALIASED imports (from ...jira_family import sanitize_label as _sanitize_label), so the module-local underscore names, __all__, and every call site stay byte-identical and the diff is provably import-lines-only."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785341751177079001,"uuid":"a2a62716-5eae-4b5d-a974-2ef202d9022c"}